<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahuas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nahua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nahuas are a group of Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> who speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. In the late pre-Hispanic period they included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> and many other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of central Mexico. They remain the largest Indigenous group in modern Mexico.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahuas.jpg" alt="Nahuas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando Rosales · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ni%C3%B1as_Nahuas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Nahua comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> word-root nāhua-, which means audible, intelligible, clear, and was used in contrast with popoloca, to speak unintelligibly. Archaeological, historical and linguistic evidence suggests that the Nahuas came from the deserts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and migrated into central Mexico in several waves. From about 600 CE the Nahuas rose quickly to power in central Mexico, expanding into areas earlier held by Oto-Manguean, Totonacan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Huastec peoples</a>, and adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> agriculture, urbanism, a ritual calendar of 260 days and monumental architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1000 CE the Toltecs, usually thought to have been Nahua, held much of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from Tollan Xicocotitlan. After their fall, large population movements followed, and groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> and Nicarao reached north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> city-states, or altepetl, contended for dominance: the Xochimilca south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanecs</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acolhua">Acolhua</a> to the east. One of the last Nahua groups to arrive, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, settled on an island in Lake Texcoco and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. They formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> after allying with the Tepanecs and the Acolhua of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>.</p><p>In 1519 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed on the Gulf coast. The Tlaxcaltecs, a Nahua group who had never been subjugated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, fought the Spaniards, were defeated and then allied with them. Spanish and Tlaxcaltec forces took cities under Aztec rule and, after the Spaniards were driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, besieged and took it. Nahua forces often formed the bulk of the Spanish expeditions against the Maya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a>. The Colegio de Santa Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> opened in 1536 to educate young Nahua noblemen as priests, ceased to work to that end by the mid-16th century, and in 1555 Indians were barred from ordination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Within twenty years of the Spanish arrival the Nahuas were writing their own language in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet. Their wills, town council records and petitions form a large body of sources, and from about 1650 they also wrote primordial titles, texts that assert a community's rights to its land. Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> before the conquest, which is why the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and several Mexican states are Nahuatl.</p><p>About 1.5 million Nahuas speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and another million speak only Spanish. Fewer than 2,000 native speakers of Nawat remain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. The Nahuas are now the largest Indigenous group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and the term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, once applied to them, has largely fallen out of favour in scholarship except for the Aztec Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nahuas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Besides the Purépecha, whose language is unrelated to any other in Mesoamerica, the kingdom included <b>Nahua</b>, Otomí, Matlatzinca and other peoples, who kept their own languages but owed tribute and, in war, warriors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their settlements grew together into a single sprawling capital spread over hilltops, terraces and valley floor, and the confederation’s territory of some 1,400 square kilometres included Otomí as well as <b>Nahua</b> towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For the Aztecs, Tollan was the model of civilisation: in Nahuatl a toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’, meant art and refinement, while the <b>Nahua</b> informants of Bernardino de Sahagún credited the Toltecs with inventing medicine and the calendar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franciscan Bernardino de Sahagún worked for decades with <b>Nahua</b> scholars to compile the Florentine Codex, completed in the 1570s, a twelve-book encyclopaedia of Aztec gods, rituals, beliefs and society, written in Nahuatl and Spanish and illustrated by Nahua artists; it remains the most important source for Aztec…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the conquest, the <b>Nahuas</b> recorded their history, tribute, calendars and rituals in painted books, the codices, in a pictorial system that combined images with glyphs for names, places and dates.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>He worked on it from 1545 until his death in 1590, in partnership with <b>Nahua</b> elders and authors who were formerly his students at the Colegio de Santa Cruz de Tlatelolco.</blockquote><a href="/work/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
